ealize the magnitude of the  kinds of deals that were happening in  the opportunity for crime of us on a  scale that had never been seen on Wall  Street before and I say in the epilogue  that there are laws that need to be  toughened on this I mean we now the  Salomon scandal is raging as long as  Wall Street traders measure risk and  reward and decide that the rewards  outweigh the risk they're going to do  things are going to break the law and so  the risk of getting caught has to be  made clear I do think we need a  definition of insider trading and I  think the financial penalties in these  crimes need to be increased the press  yes what do you think of the press's  performers during this whole story well  I was part of it I have to admit and  then I I guess I I'd like to say that I  was particularly proud of the The Wall  Street Journal slugging away on this and  trying to write the facts I mean I I can  tell you that there were numerous  numerous attempts by powerful people in  Wall Street to get me and at my  colleague at the time Dan  Hertzberg not only off this story but  fired from our jobs and the chairman of  dal Jones Warren Phillips and my boss  Norm prole Stein staunchly supported us  during all of this and there was a great  deal of good coverage that came out of  the press now was there also some  members of the press who were all too  easily manipulated by the public  relations forces I believe there were  there were some who for ideological  reasons wanted to believe the Milken law  and whatever the whatever the facts  really were and then there were others  who who succumbed all too easily to  these promises of access that were to a  great figure like Milken that we're  emanating from  Robinson Lake Camp if you had more pages  to devote to all this what would you  have included in this book that you  didn't well the original manuscript did  have more pages and I I credit my editor  Alice min here with very wisely cutting  down some of this stuff 